00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:01.750 align:start position:0%
 
let's get the thoughts of labour now  we're joined once again in the studio by  the shadow working pension secretary  jonathan ashworth good to see you this  morning um  given that which we've we've just been  hearing in and around mario paul kiev  lev we are hearing any number of stories  of atrocities taking place on the ground  in ukraine do you understand why the  government feels unable to go quite as  far as the ukrainian president and  describe  these as war crimes as genocide and  indeed identify vladimir putin as  responsible for them well they are  heinous atrocities absolutely  appalling in my view there are crimes  now i'm not an international lawyer so  there may well be  particular definitions but what we are  seeing coming out of ukraine are is  absolutely the most appalling scenes  i do hold putin responsible  he needs to be held to account and the  international community need to find a  way to do that but we need to continue  to do all we can to support ukraine we  need to support the refugees  and i've got nothing but absolute  tribute to everybody who has donated to  the different charity appeals including  in my own leicester constituency where  i've been supporting it we need to do  all we can and of course we need to  ensure that the maximum pressure is put  on russia through the sanctions regime i  mean you you say that vladimir putin  indeed as did such a java this morning  that he that he must be held to account  i suppose the question is how is that  possible given you're not going to be  able to extradite them to the icc well  there will have to come a point where  the international community needs to  come together and decide how to hold him  to account but in the immediate term  we've got to support the refugees coming  out of ukraine with the proper  humanitarian assistance i know the  government has is putting in place  schemes but i've been reading that the  the charity groups and local authorities  that the government are understandably  asking for help have been left in the  lurch don't know  what what provisions are going to get  what support they're going to get so we  need clarity from government as to how  they're going to help the refugees in  the short term but of course we need to  as an international community work  together internationally to put in place  a plan in order to hold russia to  account but what then did you make of of  the prime minister's remarks to the  conservative spring conference i've  heard from the health secretary this  morning we heard from the chancellor  yesterday claiming that he was not  drawing a direct comparison between the  two he certainly mentioned them in short  in short succession well he was drawing  a direct comparison but you know what  you know why he was doing it he know  he's a clever operator he's a very good  manipulator he throws these things out  there to suck the oxygen out of the  other big issues that are around and  he's trying to distract people from the  cost of living crisis where nine million  families are going to be 500 pounds  worth of where he's imposing this  punishing tax rise  on people where pensioners are going to  lose 388 pounds and the value of the  basic state pension he's just doing it  so we all sort of have a row on that  rather than focus on the issues that are  impacting the british people day in day  out which is the fact that many of them  are facing starving or freezing in the  words of martin lewis the money-saving  expert i i want to talk about that in a  bit more depth in just a second but  let's talk about something completely on  your briefing the situation as regards  piano ferries 800 individuals fired with  apparently no warning  what is it that the labourer are trying  to seek in the commons today is this  just a rerun of the efforts made by  barry gardner beforehand to get you know  fire and rehard as a practice band we  need action from the government i mean  the way piano have acted uh piano  ferries have acted towards their uh uh  seafaring workforce areas beyond content  it's absolutely disgusting  is it legal though uh by your  understanding is it illegal because  again what neither of us are lawyers  there are there are plenty of lawyers  who suggest that it perhaps wasn't well  but that's why we need clarity from the  government as i understand it the  government knew this was going to happen  and didn't tell anyone i mean what are  they playing at by the way i've got  nothing but tribute for the rmt union  and carl turner he's one of the local  mps and actually louis hague my  colleague the shadow transport secretary  has been they have all been superb in  fighting for these workers but these  practices should be outlawed and the  government the business secretary should  be looking into the legalities of it he  should be if they believe it is illegal  looking at criminal proceedings against  the uh  the bosses of this firm and the and if  there's no good tory mps ringing their  hand saying he's all terrible many of  them voted against the legislation which  a labour mp brought forward to ban these  fire and rehire practices and they  should vote with us today in the house  of commons to ban these fire and rehire  type practices but what what can the  government do bar wholesale changes to  legislation about you know a company  like p o ferries hiring via foreign  agencies individuals on less than the  minimum wage  they need to stump it out they need to  stamp it out they don't need to parade  around the studios after this morning oh  we'll say it's terrible i was shocked by  the shocked when i saw it on my on the  screens as well you know they're the  government they can do something about  it and tory mps can do something about  it so they should vote with us today to  send a message to the business secretary  and others in the cabinet that they need  to fix this  just a quick word then from what you  would like to see from from the  chancellor come wednesday his hands are  are not exactly tied there's quite a bit  of extra cash flowing floating around in  the treasure at the moment thanks to the  rise in the cost of living and the extra  tax take that they're taking so where  should he be spending well look you know  we've had 12 years of economic  mismanagement which has left us so  exposed to surging inflation and that  now means we've got rising energy bills  so unaffordable we've got tax rises so  punishing universal credit cuts so  severe basic state pension cuts so  devastating that people as martin luther  says are facing a choice between  starving or freezing he should increase  the uh he shouldn't be cutting benefits  like the pension and universal credit he  shouldn't be going ahead with this big  national insurance rise a 10  increase he could put a windfall tax on  the oil and gas producers to fund 600  pounds for off the of the heating bills  for many households so there is action  the chancellor should take and he should  take it in his money budget on wednesday  well we'll find out if he's been  listening to you then uh jonathan  ashworth thanks for joining us thanks a  lot

